# Native occupancy and directory gates — offline preparation

These sources are deliberately disabled for production execution by the literal
`DEVICE_EXECUTION_ENABLED = 0`. Environment variables and compiler flags do not
enable that path. Host tests build a separate fixture-only path. No Android,
ADB, USB, phone SSH or target execution was used. Initial tests ran on macOS;
a subsequent existing local Colima VM performed Linux AArch64 compilation and
synthetic host tests only.

The reviewed caller must pin source/build artifacts, hold its transaction lock,
bind a fresh boot ID and an independent 32-character operation ID, and record
the raw JSONL. Neither helper is a standalone authorization mechanism. A new
enabled build, integration review and device validation would still be needed.

## Read-only scanner

```
native_occupancy_scanner scan TXID BOOT_UUID OPID32 DEADLINE_BOOTTIME_MS
```

TXID is exactly 16 lowercase hex characters; OPID32 is 32 lowercase hex
characters. The deadline is absolute `CLOCK_BOOTTIME` milliseconds, greater
than current time and at most 7,000 ms away. A host deadline of 8 seconds is
separate. Host tests use C/Python `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` explicitly; Python's generic
`time.monotonic()` is not assumed to have the same epoch on macOS.

The scanner reads `/proc` and each leader's `task` list, checks every discovered
TID's starttime and kernel-thread flag, deduplicates mountinfo by mount namespace
identity, and inspects every TID's FD links, cwd and root. It checks both the
mount root and mount point after decoding Linux mountinfo escapes. It also
checks global loop backing paths. Protected paths are the four exact roles:

* `/data/e5linux`
* `/data/e5linux.stage.TXID`
* `/data/e5linux.archive.TXID`
* `/data/e5linux.failed.TXID`

The corresponding filesystem-root paths (`/e5linux...`) are recognized too.
The scanner walks existing role trees without reading contents, records their
device/inode pairs, and checks FD and loop aliases against those pairs. A role
tree must be on the same filesystem, with only directories and singleton
regular files. Known paths including a deleted suffix are busy.

Limits are 4,096 distinct task IDs, 131,072 FD observations, 256 distinct mount
namespaces, 1 MiB per mountinfo file, 32 MiB of total text reads, 128 loop nodes,
1,024 protected tree entries and depth 16. Exceeding a limit is failure, not
partial success. Fields called `processes` in the v1 JSON count checked TIDs.
Kernel threads may lack namespace/cwd/root links only when PF_KTHREAD and
stable starttime are known and their FD list is empty. An unconfigured loop is
accepted only if the loop node exists and its entire loop-attribute directory
is absent; a missing backing file inside an existing attribute directory fails.

Task lists are enumerated again at the end. A disappearing process/FD, changed
task set, changed PID starttime or mount namespace, malformed/unreadable input,
busy target, deadline or cancellation yields an incomplete result. There is no
automatic retry and no race exception that silently skips a process. A natural
exit is not itself a device fault; it simply makes this scan inconclusive.

JSONL schema `e5-native-occupancy-v1` emits `BEGIN`, `PHASE`, final `RESULT` with
txid/opid/boot_id/self_pid/self_starttime/deadline, coverage counts, failure phase,
PID/FD and `unknown`. Accept only exit 0, a single final `RESULT`, matching all
bindings and `complete: true`, `unknown: []`. It is explicitly **not an atomic
snapshot** or proof that no process can open the directory after the scan.
The caller must keep exclusive ownership and recheck immediately before a
directory mutation, along with its independent tree/manifest/boot checks.

## Directory seal / no-replace rename

```
device_directory_helper ACTION TXID BOOT_UUID OPID32 DEADLINE_BOOTTIME_MS DATA_DEV DATA_INO SOURCE_DEV SOURCE_INO ROLE
```

Actions are exactly `seal`, `move-active-to-archive`, `move-stage-to-active`,
`move-active-to-failed`, `move-archive-to-active`. ROLE must be the exact source
role (or selected role for seal). All actions, including seal, require the same
maximum 7-second absolute deadline. The fixed directory names, layouts,
device/inode matching, singleton-file rules, 256 entries/depth 8 limits,
file/directory/parent fsync and `RENAME_NOREPLACE`/`RENAME_EXCL` behavior are
retained. Unsupported no-replace operations stop without fallback.

TERM, INT, HUP and PIPE set a cancellation flag. Every tree iteration and each
fsync has control checks before and after it. Boot ID is checked before opening,
before rename and before a success result. Immediately before rename, the
helper blocks cancellation signals briefly, examines pending cancellation,
checks the deadline, and calls no-replace once. It records whether the syscall
was attempted and whether it returned success before unblocking signals.

Cancellation observed before this final admission prevents rename. A signal
that races an already committed syscall cannot undo it: the tool returns an
error with `mutation_attempted: true`, and `renamed: true` when success was
observed. It never retries or rolls back. Failed/lost replies after mutation
admission require a new read-only inspection; `renamed: false` alone does not
prove an attempted syscall had no effect.

JSONL schema `e5-native-directory-v3` emits `BEGIN`, `PHASE`, final `RESULT`.
Accept only exit 0, matching bindings and final `ok: true`,
`fsync_completed: true`, `outcome: COMPLETED`. Any post-admission failure is
`UNKNOWN_AFTER_MUTATION_ATTEMPT`. A successful fsync sequence is not an empirical
power-loss guarantee (`power_loss_safety_proven: false`). A blocked kernel I/O
may outlive the host timeout or resist cancellation: the host must retain
UNKNOWN and establish process quiescence before another writer. These helpers
do not bypass a watchdog or assert that killing a host process killed Android.

## Host validation and limits

`test_native_occupancy_scanner.py`: 35 synthetic proc/sys fixture tests.
`test_device_directory_helper.py`: 37 real temporary-filesystem tests.
Both compile with `-std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -O2`, test disabled production
entrypoints and restrict subprocesses to the compiler and produced host test
binaries. Tests include actual TERM/INT/HUP delivery, elapsed native deadlines,
alias/namespace checks, unknown task/FD coverage, no-replace and fsync failures,
and post-rename cancellation with preserved moved state.

Scanner fixtures are only canonical `/tmp/e5-native-test-*/sandbox` (Darwin
`/private/tmp` spelling accepted). Helper fixtures are only canonical
`/tmp/e5-directory-test-*/data`. Test boot IDs are fixture files and test
`self_starttime` is explicitly synthetic 1. Fault hooks exist only in host-test
builds. No host test measures Android scan latency, kernel/fsync cancellation,
power loss, F2FS durability or integration with the eventual live adapter.

## Linux build and single-chunk I/O integration

`research/author-clean-reinstall-20261014/helper-build-v3/build.json` binds
three static AArch64 **DISABLED** executables, source hashes, compiler arguments,
ELF inspection, exit-70 disabled-entry probes and Linux test logs. The same
final sources pass 39 I/O + 35 scanner + 37 directory tests on macOS and Linux.
The Linux build required only GCC warning cleanup in addition to the substantive
cancellation gates; no warning was disabled. Production constant folding can
remove unreachable implementations, so these disabled binaries do not prove
algorithm execution; separate fixture-only builds exercise the algorithms.

`native_io.c` is the parent's fixed single-chunk primitive. During review we
added final signal/deadline admission around exclusive file creation and the
single pwrite: pending cancellation refuses the syscall, while signals racing
an already committed syscall yield ERROR / `possibly_modified: true`. Six new
tests cover these boundaries. Its 6-second limit, fixed eight stage files,
4 MiB chunks, exact p37 B slot and misc p3 offset 2048 length 32 remain unchanged.
Because this reviewer contributed that patch, the final I/O source requires a
separate review and is not presented as independently reviewed here.
